\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RCNmodADJP1
\item[* relevant examples:] nice dress, old cheese, etc.
\item[* language:] English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] NP-subgrammar
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) mod1rules
\item[* date of creation:] 28-FEB-1988 09:28:19  
\item[* author:] Franciska de Jong
\item[* copied from:]
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 28/10/88\ \ \ \ \ author: Lisette Appelo
\item date: 02/11/88\ \ \ \ \ author: Lisette Appelo
\item date: 28/02/90\ \ \ \ \ author: Lisette Appelo\\
Added temporal conditions. The most left ADJP is dominant in this respect.
Rule had to be split up, because EN has no attributes.
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] Modification of a CN by an atributive ADJP(PROP).
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
The CN is the substituent (the argument) of the ADJPPROP (the function), 
i.e. the CN is substituted for the VAR (= CNVAR) of the ADJPPROP.
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
