\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
\begin{verbatim}
There are several possibilities to make the following definite CNs:
 'vele (leuke) ideeën'     via NPformation: 'de' vele (leuke) ideeën.
 'verschillende ....                        'de' verschillende ....
 'enkele ....                               'de' enkele ....
?'verscheidene ....                        ?'de' verscheidene ....
  etc.

1) If we consider these words NUMs, the fit into the rule above.
2) If we consider them ADJs, they run parallel to other ADJs.
3) If we consider them DETs, the rule above has to be adapted as to include
   these DETs as well:
        m1: DETP{DETPrec1}[head/DET{DETrec1}
    with certain conditions, e.g.:
                    DETrec1.definite = indef   ( NB. *de menige N  
                                                     *de sommige N  
                                                     *de alle N     )
It is not clear to me yet if all indefinite DETs allow 'de' though.

NB. Consider 'veel' vs. 'vele':
              - veel/vele boeken.
              - de vele boeken.
               *de veel boeken.
     1) 'veel' = DET
        'vele' = NUM   ( NB. Compare:   'enkele' boeken    DET
                                        de 'enkele' boeken NUM  ???? )
     2) 'veel' = DET
        'vele' = DET / ADJ
     3) both DETs, 'veel' having the attribute value 'veeldef';
                   'vele' having the attribute value 'indef'.
\end{verbatim}
\item modifications: 28/10/88: Added sup[erdeixis for DETP.\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% RULE RCNmodposs1


< m1: CN{CNrec1}[I1:mu1] 

  m2: NP{NPrec1}[mu2, 
                 head/T1,
                 mu3
                ]
>

< m : CN{CNrec1}[I1:mu1,
                 posrel/PREPP{PREPPrec1}
                       [head/PREP(KEY_ofprepkey){PREPrec1},
                        objrel/I2::NP{NPrec2}
                              [mu2,
                               head/T2,
                               mu3
                              ]
                       ]     
                ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      < I1:  NOT EXIST (mu1, [head/EN{ENrec1}])  AND
             NOT EXIST (mu1, [posrel/T3]) AND
             NOT EXIST (mu1, [postmodrel/PROPERNOUN{PROPERNOUNrec2}[mu7]])
        m2: NPrec1.generic = nogeneric AND
            NPrec1.cases = []
        I2: NPrec2.generic = omegageneric AND
            accusative IN NPrec2.cases 
      >

<
(* 1 *) 
SUBRULE          (*  (book of) my father *)

<T1: CN{CNrec2}[mu4]>
<T2: CN{CNrec3}[mu4]>


      COMP
         <
          C1: NPrec1.superdeixis = CNrec1.superdeixis AND
              CNrec2.cases = []
          A1: NPrec2 := NPrec1;
              NPrec2.superdeixis := omegadeixis;
              NPrec2.generic := omegageneric;
              NPrec2.cases := [accusative];
              CNrec3 := CNrec2;
              CNrec3.cases := [accusative]
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: NPrec2.superdeixis = omegadeixis AND
              accusative IN CNrec3.cases
          A1: NPrec1 := NPrec2;
              NPrec1.superdeixis := CNrec1.superdeixis;
              NPrec1.generic := nogeneric;
              NPrec1.cases := []; 
              CNrec2 := CNrec3;
              CNrec2.cases := []
         >

(* 2 *) 
SUBRULE            (*  (book of) mine *)

<T1: PERSPRO{PERSPROrec1}[head/BPERSPRO{BPERSPROrec1}]>
<T2: POSSPRO{POSSPROrec1}[head/BPERSPRO{BPERSPROrec1}]>


      COMP
         <
          C1: NPrec1.superdeixis = CNrec1.superdeixis AND
              PERSPROrec1 = COPYT_bpersproTOperspro(BPERSPROrec1) AND
              PERSPROrec1.persprocase = omegacase AND
              BPERSPROrec1.predpos = true
          A1: NPrec2 := NPrec1;
              NPrec2.superdeixis := omegadeixis;
              NPrec2.generic := omegageneric;
              NPrec2.cases := [accusative]; 
              (* POSSPRO has no attributes *)
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: BPERSPROrec1.predpos = true AND
              NPrec2.superdeixis = omegadeixis 
          A1: PERSPROrec1 := COPYT_bpersproTOperspro(BPERSPROrec1);
              PERSPROrec1.persprocase := omegacase;
              NPrec1 := NPrec2;
              NPrec1.superdeixis := CNrec1.superdeixis;
              NPrec1.generic := nogeneric;
              NPrec1.cases := []; 
         >

(* 3 *) 
SUBRULE        (* (book of) everyone *)

<T1: INDEFPRO{INDEFPROrec1}[mu5]>
<T2: INDEFPRO{INDEFPROrec1}[mu5]>


      COMP
         <
          C1: NPrec1.superdeixis = CNrec1.superdeixis
          A1: NPrec2 := NPrec1;
              NPrec2.superdeixis := omegadeixis;
              NPrec2.generic := omegageneric;
              NPrec2.cases := [accusative]; 
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: NPrec2.superdeixis = omegadeixis
          A1: NPrec1 := NPrec2;
              NPrec1.superdeixis := CNrec1.superdeixis;
              NPrec1.generic := nogeneric;
              NPrec1.cases := []; 
         >

(* 4 *) 
SUBRULE           (* (book of) John *)

<T1: PROPERNOUN{PROPERNOUNrec1}[mu6]>
<T2: PROPERNOUN{PROPERNOUNrec1}[mu6]>


      COMP
         <
          C1: NPrec1.superdeixis = CNrec1.superdeixis
          A1: NPrec2 := NPrec1;
              NPrec2.superdeixis := omegadeixis;
              NPrec2.generic := omegageneric;
              NPrec2.cases := [accusative]; 
         >

(*  in case the suggestion on .poss is worked out (cf. remarks):

             C11: PROPERNOUN.subc IN [city, country] 
                    (ivm. 
                         the king of Denmark's subjects )
             A11: CNrec4 := CNrec1;
             C11: NOT (PROPERNOUN.subc IN [city, country]  )
             A11: CNrec4 := CNrec1;
                  CNrec4.poss := false;
*)

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: NPrec2.superdeixis = omegadeixis
          A1: NPrec1 := NPrec2;
              NPrec1.superdeixis := CNrec1.superdeixis;
              NPrec1.generic := nogeneric;
              NPrec1.cases := []; 
         >
>
&


\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] CNmodposs1
\item[* relevant examples:]\mbox{}
Subrule 1: book my father $\rightarrow$ book of my father\\
subrule 2: book I $\rightarrow$ book of mine\\
subrule 3: book everyone $\rightarrow$ book of everyone\\
subrule 4: book John $\rightarrow$ book of John
\item[* language:] English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] CN-subgrammar
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) CNmodpossrules
\item[* date of creation:] 15-MAR-1988 11:08:41  
\item[* author:] Franciska de Jong
\item[* copied from:]
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 17/11/88\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted all subrules for cases of NPrec2 (all becoming [accusative]; it is 
assumed the introduction of the PREP demands immediate case-assignment). Also 
adapted subrule 2 to form POSSPROs out of the PERSPRO. A POSSPRO has no record, 
so no attributes need to be set. Note that the genitive
{\em 's\/} needed in the other subrules (or at least in nrs.\ 1 and 4) has {\em 
not\/} been provided for yet. Finally adjusted the model, so that there is a T1 
in the left model, and a T2 in the right model. This is necessary since in 
subrule 2, the input tree is changed.
\item date: 02/08/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted subrule 1 to reset cases of CN as well (needed for NPformation)
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] 
Introduction of postnominal possessive `of'-modifiers.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
\begin{enumerate}
\item PERSPRO's, WHPRO's, INDEFPRO's, PROPERNOUNs and EN's are not dominated 
by CN.
\item Only {\em one} posrel can occur in a CN: \\
      MATCHCONDITIONS \\
      ( NOT EXIST (mu1, {\em posrel}/T2) )
\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{} Possibly the CN resulting from this rule should 
have the value {\em false} for {\bf .poss}. 
This would require not only  the distinction
of CNrec1 and e.g. CNrec4, but also the introduction of the head NOUN  in the 
model (reversibility requires that in analysis the value of {\bf .poss}
is reset to the corresponding value of the head NOUN).
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
it is not clear yet whether there are conditions on mu2.

(NPformation can make {\em the\/} book of my father('s), semantically equivalent 
to {\em my father's book\/}).

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

